+.SH BINFMT_MISC
+The guest sees a directory
+.I /proc/sys/fs/binfmt_misc
+with the usual
+.IR register ,
+.I status
+and one file per entry, so tools such as update-binfmts or qemu-binfmt-conf.sh
+work. Rules use the kernel syntax
+.RI ( :name:type:offset:magic:mask:interpreter:flags
+with
+.B \\xHH
+escapes, type
+.B M
+or
+.BR E )
+and are honoured by every execve made by guest programs, before #! and ELF
+handling; the newest matching enabled rule wins. Writing
+.BR 0 ,
+.B 1
+or
+.B \-1
+to an entry disables, enables or removes it, and to
+.I status
+does so for all entries. Rules persist per container.
+.PP
+Flags \fBP\fR (preserve argv[0]), \fBO\fR, \fBC\fR and \fBF\fR
+are accepted; the binary is always passed to the interpreter by path (there is no
+AT_EXECFD), and the interpreter is looked up when the program is run, not when it is
+registered. An interpreter that is a static executable cannot translate paths, so it
+receives the host path of the program.
+.PP
+Registrations are processed the next time the guest opens a path below the
+directory or runs a program, so an invalid or duplicate line is silently ignored
+instead of failing the
+.BR write (2).
+Programs that bypass the shim (static binaries, raw syscalls) are not affected by
+the rules when they call execve themselves.
